 "strings": {
  "When did we become\na couple of old geezers?": "",
  "Agh, my gut's achin'\nfor some bakin'!": "",
  "Maybe I'll stop in for a bite?": "",
  "Caught a long'un today!": "",
  "Ah, scrumptious, that!\nThanks as always!": "",
  "Girl was a right minx, she was!": "",
  "That's downright amazing!": "",
  "Suppose it musta\nslipped me mind!": "",
  "I got more'n my share\na' troubles an' woe...": "",
  "I'll believe that far\nas I can throw it!": "",
  "That's how I sees it!": "",
  "When you plan to quit\nthat lousy habit?": "",
  "What's that got\nto do with anythin'?": "",
  "That's a good look on ya!": "",
  "Fancy that one, do you?": "",
  "Da once said\nthe tough times'll pass...": "",
  "Fancy I should take a peek?": "",
  "Where d'ya think\nyou're runnin' off to!?": "",
  "Oi, hold on a minute there!": "",
  "Y'got wax in your\nears or somethin'?": "",
  "*sigh* I just can't keep up\nwith the young'uns anymore.": "",
  "*sigh* Ain't this\na load a' shit?": "",
  "I can handle it, all right?": "",
  "Woohoo!": "",
  "Oi, whatcha laughin' about!?": "",
  "Heeheehee!": "",
  "Ahahahaha! What a pisser!": "",
  "That sounds like a\nright what-have-you!": "",
  "Now ain't that\na pain-in-the-ass!": "",
  "Is that right?": "",
  "Sure to be lonely\n'round here for a bit.": "",
  "Aye, I needs be\ngettin' home soon.": "",
  "Ah well, crack on then.": "",
  "Oh please, boyo!": "",
  "Who's up for some dukes, eh?": "",
  "Come back again, lads!": "",
  "'Ey, still gotcher\nhealth, ain't you?": "",
  "Cheeky!": "",
  "Ah, bugger me!": "",
  "Jus' need to put\nour spines into it!": "",
  "Enough a' that!": "",
  "Now that's lovely!": "",
  "Now that's brilliant!": "",
  "It's been all too long!": "",
  "You're always welcome\n'round here, boyo!": "",
  "Let's get together again soon!": "",
  "Heheh, luck done\nsmiled on me t'day!": "",
  "You been keepin' well?": "",
  "Ain't seen you in a dog's age!": "",
  "Cut me a bit of slack!": "",
  "Reckon I could get it back...": "",
  "It's all good, ain't it?": "",
  "You 'right, mate?": "",
  "Whose treat? Yours?": "",
  "I missed you\nsomethin' awful, I did!": "",
  "Got meself good an' pissed\nlast night, I did.": "",
  "What's that?\nYou takin' the mick?": "",
  "That's a well\npopular one lately.": "",
  "What's been crackin' lately?": "",
  "Ma always said don't\nsweat the small stuff.": "",
  "Have I got a story for ye!": "",
  "My stomach's howlin'!": "",
  "Pick up your chin, lad!": "",
  "Thanks for lendin' me an ear.": "",
  "I was fixin' to buy it,\nbut I got caught short!": "",
  "Oi, give us a song!": "",
  "You've had more'n\nyour fill of booze!": "",
  "Win": "",
  "Loss": "",
  "In Buddhism, it refers to a comfortable world without suffering. ": "",
  "Rather than serving as group leader, this position is second in charge of an organization. The name is derived from the notion that this person would be the first to sign their name on any group documents.": "",
  "The inheriting head of a family or other clan that passes through generations, including schools of swordsmanship.": "",
  "Udon noodle soup topped with bits of fried tempura batter. There are a range of theories regarding the name; some believe it is inspired by the legendary animal, while others believe it to be a corruption of some other word or phrase.": "",
  "Udon noodle soup topped with seasoned, deep-fried tofu. Cultural belief in the kitsune's love for this topping led it to become the dish's namesake.": "",
  "Udon noodle soup topped with a raw egg. As the egg cooks in the hot broth, it develops into a bright round circle that resembles the moon, giving the dish its namesake \"moon viewing.\"": "",
  "Ee Ja Nai Ka ": "",
  "Karakuri": "",
  "Political activists during the late Edo period, usually involved in anti-Bakufu activities.": "",
  "Virtue ": "",
  "Dosukoi!": "",
  "Fundoshi": "",
  "A Western approach to medicine introduced to Japan by the Dutch. Differed greatly from the nation's own approach to healthcare at the time, which combined faith-based practices and aspects of traditional Chinese medicine. ": "",
  "A natural fiber made from the cocoons of Japanese oak silkmoths.": "",
  "Hitotsubashi": "",
  "A samurai with a rank between goshi and joshi. This rank was exclusively bestowed to Tosa Domain goshi and their families for notable achievements.": "",
  "The process of reformation and rebirth, restoration, or revolution.": "",
  "A term used to refer to European countries, often Portugal. [Nanban as a term originated from the Chinese term referring to those of the south, such as the current South East Asian countries. This term was borrowed by the Japanese, and eventually included the Portuguese and other Europeans, and started being used as a term for those that came from outside of Japan.] ": "",
  "Kyo ": "",
  "The northeast region of Kyo. Became known as the Fushimi Ward after the city was renamed \"Kyoto.\"": "",
  "The area lying within Kyo's city limits.": "",
  "The suburban area surrounding Kyo, not counting the city itself.": "",
  "A shopping and entertainment district with bustling streets typical to Kyo.": "",
  "Aizu Domain": "",
  "A domain located in the northeast of Honshu. The area is part of the current-day Fukushima Prefecture.": "",
  "A sword that is over 90 centimeters (nearly three feet) in length.": "",
  "A sword that is between between 30 and 60 centimeters (around one to two feet) in length. ": "",
  "Seppuku": "",
  "Loyalist": "",
  "Nanki": "",
  "Nanban ": "",
  "Doshin": "",
  "A traditional Japanese overcoat, normally hip- or waist-length. Once only worn by the warrior classes, the Edo period saw some commoners starting to wear haori to formal occasions as well.": "",
  "A domain in Shikoku, north of the Tosa Domain. The area is part of the current-day Ehime Prefecture.": "",
  "A province northeast of Tosa. The area is part of the current-day Tokushima Prefecture.": "",
  "\"Sonno\" is a declaration that the foremost leader of the nation is the Emperor, as opposed to the Bakufu.\n\n\"Joui\" is a demand to drive out all foreigners and refuse them access to the nation.": "",
  "Ozu": "",
  "Tairo": "",
  "Tenchu": "",
  "Okami ": "",
  "Ansei Purge": "",
  "Heir": "",
  "An adult son who is ineligible to inherit his family's title, yet continues to live in his parents' house without attaining independence. ": "",
  "Sun / Shaku": "",
  "Koban": "",
  "Kodachi ": "",
  "Kodachi Handling ": "",
  "A domain encompassing much of Shikoku's southern region, ruled by the Yamauchi clan. The area is now known as the Kochi Prefecture.": "",
  "Shrine Maiden ": "",
  "A lower-ranking Bakufu official typically assigned to law enforcement. Employed by the various officials of Kyo, they were in charge of policing the majority of the city's criminal activity.": "",
  "A political group founded in the Tosa Domain near the end of the Edo period.": "",
  "A term of respect for a woman running her own business. ": "",
  "A top-level Bakufu officer permitted direct contact with the Shogun. In the Sengoku period, they were the samurai serving as the closest bodyguards for their lords. By the Edo period, they were given a much wider range of duties and were often awarded land. ": "",
  "Three Houses of the Tokugawa": "",
  "The three Tokugawa family clans of highest nobility and the only heirs permitted to claim the Tokugawa name. They consist of the Owari, the Kishu, and the Mito clans, each ruling over their namesake domains.": "",
  "Shishi": "",
  "Ebisu": "",
  "An action taken by someone who believes they are fulfilling the will of the heavens as a form of \"divine punishment.\"": "",
  "Sengoku Period": "",
  "A clan of samurai led by their namesake lord who united the Shikoku lands during the Sengoku period. When the Yamauchi clan was granted the Tosa domain, many former Chosokabe samurai were appointed the rank of goshi, while the samurai under Yamauchi were appointed the rank of joshi.": "",
  "A person residing in a domain besides their own. While the term is regularly used with samurai, it is not exclusive to their class.": "",
  "The military government led by the Shogun. Often referred to as \"the shogunate\" in Western writing.": "",
  "Mon / Ryo": "",
  "Hatamoto": "",
  "Mechanical contraptions built using historic engineering methods.": "",
  "Equivalent to a pub, it is a bar that serves a variety of food as well.": "",
  "Traditional Japanese underwear. Comprised of a strip of cloth connected to a waist strap so that the wearer must step into the leg holes to don the underwear.": "",
  "A sweet beverage typically made from fermented rice. The alcohol content can range from low to none, depending on production methods.": "",
  "One of the Seven Gods of Fortune in Japanese culture. He is typically depicted with a fishing rod in his right hand and a sea bream tucked under his left arm.": "",
  "A war-torn era of Japan's history that began in the late 15th century and lasted well over a hundred years.": "",
  "A high-end restaurant that serves classical Japanese cuisine. They usually have a restricted clientele list, accepting new customers by referral only.": "",
  "A major city in central Japan, later renamed \"Tokyo.\" It was already one of the largest metropolises in the world by the end of the Edo period.": "",
  "Sakurada Gate": "",
  "Sukhavati ": "",
  "The act of samurai cutting down passersby on the streets, in order to test the sharpness of their blades. Basically street murder. ": "",
  "A high-ranking samurai.": "",
  "A low-ranking samurai.": "",
  "A place to practice martial arts. ": "",
  "Mito Domain": "",
  "Edo": "",
  "One of the innermost gates protecting Edo Castle and the site of Ii Naosuke's assassination at the hands of ronin samurai.": "",
  "A red-light district officially sanctioned by the Edo Bakufu.": "",
  "The highest position grantable in the Bakufu. They worked closely with their appointing Shoguns, and would even take over as temporary leaders in times where the Shogun could not serve.": "",
  "An oval-shaped gold coin used throughout the Edo period. Despite their size shrinking over the centuries, they kept a consistent exchange rate of one ryo to one koban until their use was discontinued.": "",
  "Units of measurement commonly used in Japan during the Edo period. 1 shaku = 10 sun = ~30.3 centimeters (~11.93 inches).": "",
  "A samurai's self-exile from his domain, with punishment for returning potentially including death. ": "",
  "A social movement during the late Edo period where commoners created seemingly-pointless public disturbances. Crowds would parade through town, dancing and chanting \"Ee ja nai ka\" (roughly translating to \"Why not?\" or \"Who cares?\"). Some believe this was a form of protest, but no explicit purpose has ever been discovered. These incidents sometimes resulted in nearby crimes of opportunity, such as burglary.": "",
  "Currency used in Japan during the Edo period. The exchange rate for ryo (gold coins) and mon (silver coins) fluctuated daily. By the end of the period, the prevailing market rate was over 8,000 mon for a single ryo.": "",
  "Uraga": "",
  "An individual or like-minded group who is loyal first and foremost to the Emperor of Japan.": "",
  "Currently named \"Kyoto,\" this was the city where the nation's Emperors held residence for nearly eleven centuries.": "",
  "A domain located in the southwest of Honshu. The area is part of the current-day Yamaguchi Prefecture.": "",
  "A port town located where the Uraga Channel meets the Tokyo Bay. The area is now part of Yokosuka, in the current-day Kanagawa Prefecture.": "",
  "A domain consisting of the lands within the current-day Ibaraki Prefecture area. Its rulers comprised one of the Three Houses of the Tokugawa, although members of the Mito house were ineligible to become Shogun.": "",
  "A domain located in the south of Kyushu. The area is part of the current-day Kagoshima Prefecture.": "",
  "Kawaraban": "",
  "Illustrations of a sexually arousing nature, including various forms of intercourse.": "",
  "A newspaper with reports on recent events, often featuring detailed maps or other illustrations. Vendors sold these streetside, routinely announcing the latest headlines for passersby.": "",
  "High Goshi": "",
  "Bon Dance": "",
  "The traditional dance performed during the Bon festival, held in the middle of August in memory of those who have passed. The specific dance moves and accompanying music vary by region.": "",
  "Meaning \"large\" or \"thick,\" this expression comes from a popular song in sumo jinku (sumo folk music). Wrestlers publicly perform these songs during special exhibitions and ceremonies.": "",
  "Shinto Priest ": "",
  "Blessings of good fortune and favor received after doing work in the name of Buddha and the gods. ": "",
  "Known as \"kannushi\" in Japanese, these priests perform rituals dedicated to the Shinto gods while also maintaining their shrines' upkeep. ": "",
  "Known as \"miko\" in Japanese, these women perform various duties at Shinto shrines. These include aiding in sacred rites, offering prayers, performing ceremonial songs and dances, and fortune-telling. They have also served more shamanistic roles during earlier periods of Japanese history.": "",
  "A nomadic monk in the Fuke sect of Zen Buddhism. Unlike other monks, instead of shaving their heads, they wear braided straw hats concealing their faces. Their practices include playing meditative shakuhachi solos in exchange for donations.": "",
  "Representative": "",
  "Ishin": "",
  "Haori": "",
  "Swordsmanship that utilizes the wakizashi (a spare katana, or a short sword).": "",
  "Dappan ": "",
  "A method of suicide initially practiced solely by samurai wherein one slices open one's own abdomen with a short sword.": "",
  "A traditional style of dance. Unlike kabuki and noh, women were not prohibited from performing buyo.": "",
  "Satsuma": "",
  "Term used when talking about both the Satsuma and Choshu Domains. ": "",
  "Satcho ": "",
  "Dutch Medicine ": "",
  "Naval craft built in the Western world. The methods of their construction involved painting the hulls with black pitch, leading to impressionable, intimidating designs that greatly contrasted Japanese shipbuilding techniques.": "",
  "Street Killing ": "",
  "Dojo ": "",
  "Heyazumi ": "",
  "Goshi": "",
  "Chosokabe": "",
  "Sekigahara": "",
  "The site of a decisive battle in 1600 that led to the informal rise of the Tokugawa shogunate. The site is now part of the current-day Gifu Prefecture.": "",
  "Awa": "",
  "Black Ships": "",
  "A faction within the Tokugawa family who fought for their candidate, Tokugawa Yoshinobu, to succeed the 13th Shogun.": "",
  "A faction within the Tokugawa family who fought for their candidate, Tokugawa Iemochi, to succeed the 13th Shogun.": "",
  "A two-year period of strife beginning in 1858 where the Bakufu used lethal and non-lethal methods to silence critics of Japan's international trade treaties.": "",
